@alxhub @JoostK Any updates on this? I see that 9.0 entered in RC, meaning that our product will stop working as soon as Ivy becomes enabled by default.
Or could you perhaps share some insights on the exact cause of the issue, so if we can change something on our side to make this work.
Unfortunately the error message is not helpful at all.

Here's an example of a filed with variable renames which fails:

Fails:

```
    import { Component as n, NgModule as t } from "@angular/core";
    import { CommonModule as r } from "@angular/common";

    var o = function() {
      function t() {}
      t.decorators = [ {
        type: n,
        args: [ {
          selector: "test-input",
          template: "<label><ng-content></ng-content><input /></label>"
        } ]
      } ];
      return t;
    }();

    var e = function() {
      function n() {}
      n.decorators = [ {
        type: t,
        args: [ {
          imports: [ r ],
          declarations: [ o ],
          exports: [ o ]
        } ]
      } ];
      return n;
    }();

    export { o as TestComponent, e as TestModule };
```

And the original, which works:

```
    import { Component, NgModule } from '@angular/core';
    import { CommonModule } from '@angular/common';

    var TestComponent = /** @class */ (function () {
        function TestComponent() {
        }
        TestComponent.decorators = [
            { type: Component, args: [{
                        selector: 'test-input',
                        template: "<label><ng-content></ng-content><input /></label>"
                    }] }
        ];
        return TestComponent;
    }());

    var TestModule = /** @class */ (function () {
        function TestModule() {
        }
        TestModule.decorators = [
            { type: NgModule, args: [{
                        imports: [CommonModule],
                        declarations: [TestComponent],
                        exports: [TestComponent]
                    },] }
        ];
        return TestModule;
    }());

    export { TestComponent, TestModule };
```

@petebacondarwin Thanks for taking the time, this is great news!

Besides the variable name mangling, I found some more issues when using terser to minify the package:

  - Inheritance failed if the `_super` argument of the transformed class was renamed to something else. Interestingly this caused runtime error, rather than build error. It might be possible that the PR fixes this as well, the test reproduction repo does not contain inheritance.

  - If terser compress is enabled, it doesn't like the `join_vars`, `reduce_vars` and `sequences` settings, I had to turn them off.

  - In some cases (I think in case of static methods/properties), if the minifier removes the extra parenthesis around the transformed class,  `ngcc` will emit duplicate exports, causing build failure:

```
    var TestComponent = /** @class */ (function () {
    }());
```

vs.

```
    var TestComponent = function () {
    }();
```

@petebacondarwin Thanks for following up.
On github we only have the light version of the package, besides that we provide premium packages in private npm registries, where we wish to serve the minified code only.
I'm totally aware that minification does not obfuscate / protect the code, but it's definitely much harder to read, and that stops most of the people to mess with it - that's all we want here.

I also accept the fact that heavy minification will not be supported, I'm just talking about a few issues which seems weird to break the code and potentially might appear in uncompressed code as well.

Currently I'm working with the following terser options to generate a working bundle:

    terser({
      mangle: {
        keep_fnames: /^Mbsc/,
        module: false,
        reserved: ['_super'],
        toplevel: false,
      },
      compress: {
        join_vars: false,
        reduce_vars: false,
        sequences: false,
      },
      output: {
        wrap_iife: true,
      }
    }),
    postprocess([
      [/}\)\(\);/, '}());']
    ])
    
What I would expect to work:

1. Transpiled classes not wrapped inside extra paranthesis (so I can get rid of `wrap_iife` and post process replace)
2. `keep_fnames`: See the inner function name:

Works:

    var MbscComponent = /** @class */ (function () {
        function MbscComponent() {}
        MbscComponent.decorators = [...];
        return MbscComponent;
    }());

Does not work:

    var MbscComponent = /** @class */ (function () {
        function x() {}
        x.decorators = [...];
        return x;
    }());

3. `join_vars` - `var TestComponent1 = function () {...}(),TestComponent2 = function () {...}();` - this should work
4. `reduce_vars` - I'm not exactly sure what this does, need to check it further.
5. `sequences` - Stuff like `myFunc1(),myFunc2()` (with comma) should not cause problems either.

Note: I last tested this with rc4 I think, I will try it with the current latest rc8 and update if anything changed.

